In article <1990Mar9.164652.20063@pds3> bob@pds3.UUCP (Robert A. Earl) writes:
>By the way, when I try to compile with msc5.1, I get the "OUT OF
>[NEAR|FAR] HEAP SPACE" (depending on whether or not STUPID, STUPID_CPP,
>and other options are def'd/undef'd.  What am I doing wrong?

Your program is large (yeah, surprise...). Here's what to do:

In your MSC distribution, you will find an executable called C1L.EXE. This
is a larger version of the C1.EXE that makes up the first pass of the C
compiler. Move aside C1.EXE (to, say, C1S.EXE) and move C1L.EXE to C1.EXE.
Then do your compilation again. The message should go away.

You can leave the larger C1 in place if you like; it's just a bit larger
and slower.

This is documented in the MSC manuals.
